trace.content:
[THOUGHT] CSF JC virus PCR is negative, which makes PML highly unlikely — as CSF JCV PCR has >90% sensitivity for definite PML in immunocompromised patients with characteristic MRI findings. A negative result strongly suggests an alternative diagnosis mimicking PML radiologically. Given the patient’s Crohn disease and progressive gait ataxia, other considerations include:  
- CNS vasculitis (e.g., primary angiitis of the CNS or secondary to IBD), which can cause multifocal white matter lesions;  
- Inflammatory demyelination (e.g., MS or ADEM-like illness), though typically enhances with contrast — this scan shows no enhancement;  
- Metabolic or mitochondrial disorders (e.g., MELAS), but less likely without other systemic features;  
- Paraneoplastic or autoimmune encephalitis — e.g., anti-GAD65, anti-mGluR1, or anti-Tr/DNER antibodies are associated with cerebellar ataxia. Anti-GAD65 is notably linked to autoimmune cerebellar ataxia and can occur in association with autoimmune conditions like Crohn disease.  

Since structural imaging is abnormal but infectious (JCV) is ruled out, next step is to evaluate for autoimmune CNS inflammation. Serum and CSF autoantibody panels — especially anti-GAD65 — are high-yield and testable now.

[TEST] Serum anti-GAD65 antibody level.

